{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012RfmgGaR1opx1uh6UpAoJb","type":"tool_result","content":"1:# Chapter 3: The Ship's Company\n2:## *\"Everyone aboard has one job, and none of them is 'be in charge'\"*\n17:| ☆ Taking Your Bearings #1 | 6 min | Medium | After a brief break |\n20:| ☆ Taking Your Bearings #2 | 5 min | Medium | After a brief break |\n22:| ☆ Taking Your Bearings #3 | 5 min | Medium | After a brief break |\n30:*If you only have 15 minutes: read §6 and take Taking Your Bearings #3. Six later chapters retrieve §6 by name. Nothing else in this chapter is retrieved as often.*\n39:## 🧭 Soundings\n118:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 2 §4 — the kubelet, CRI, and the container runtime chain]*\n154:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 2 §1 — the architectural container-versus-VM contrast]*\n192:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 3 §7 — the orchestration disclaimer, resolved]*\n202:The name comes from the Greek word for helmsman or pilot; \"K8s\" is the numeronym, with eight letters between the K and the s [source: k8s-history-ten-years-2026-08-23]. The brand you're reading did not pick the maritime register to be cute about it. The subject arrived that way.\n206:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 17 §1 — the CNCF, its governance, and the cloud native definition]*\n216:So: two kinds of machine, two sets of software. That answers Soundings question 3, and it's the first thing to fix in your head, because every component name you're about to learn hangs off it.\n254:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 3 §5 — what \"front end\" actually implies about the cluster's shape]*\n260:[Omitted long matching line]\n264:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 8 — etcd backup and restore in practice]*\n265:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 12 — Secrets live in etcd, which is why encryption at rest is a separate decision you have to make]*\n273:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 7 — how the scheduler actually chooses, in detail]*\n277:[Omitted long matching line]\n279:[Omitted long matching line]\n293:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 2 §4 — the kubelet, CRI, and container runtime chain, which this section places in its architectural context]*\n303:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 5 — Pods, PodSpecs, and what \"running and healthy\" means precisely]*\n307:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 13 §5 — `crictl`, and why a node-level tool exists below the Kubernetes API]*\n315:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 9 — Services, and how kube-proxy implements them]*\n333:[Omitted long matching line]\n337:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings: The Ship's Company\n392:- **B and C are wrong** — plausible-sounding Kubernetes-flavored packaging that the documentation never describes. If you picked one of these, the useful lesson is that \"sounds like how Kubernetes would do it\" is not a reliable guide.\n404:**If you scored 0–2:** Go back to §2 and §3 and rebuild the census from the ★ Fixed Point, then re-read the Dead Reckoning block. Ten minutes. The rest of the chapter assumes you can place any component on the right side of the control-plane/node line without thinking about it.\n414:🗺️ **Chapter 3 · Voyage Progress:** census complete → now the arrangement → then the loop\n438:> ⚠ **Navigational Hazards:** Two very common wrong beliefs live here, and they're both cheap points on exam day.\n454:[Omitted long matching line]\n456:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 9 — CoreDNS as the cluster DNS addon, and the Service DNS records it serves]*\n457:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 10 — an Ingress with no Ingress controller does nothing at all: the same pattern, first recurrence]*\n458:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 13 — `kubectl top` with no metrics-server installed]*\n459:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 17 — VPA is an add-on and is not shipped by default]*\n490:[Omitted long matching line]\n498:Now consider what that means for two components that appear to cooperate. When the scheduler assigns a Pod to a node and the kubelet on that node starts the containers, it *looks* like a handoff. It looks like the scheduler told the kubelet to do something. It didn't. The scheduler recorded a decision. The kubelet, independently, was watching for exactly that kind of record, saw it, and acted. Neither one knows the other exists.\n500:> ⚓ **Worth Securing:** The coordination mechanism in Kubernetes is **watching, not telling.** Components don't send each other instructions. They observe shared state and act on what they see. That single sentence explains most of what looks mysterious about Kubernetes' behavior, and it's the sentence Chapter 15 will retrieve when a tool called Argo CD sits outside the cluster watching a Git repository, because that's the same architecture with a different thing in the hub position.\n502:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 15 — the same shape, with a Git repository where etcd sits here]*\n509:2. From that moment, the description is simply part of the cluster's state, visible to anything watching.\n512:5. Status flows back the same way, through the API server, into etcd, where anything watching can see it.\n518:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 8 — the authentication, authorization, and admission gates a request actually passes through on its way in]*\n519:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 4 — how you write the description that gets submitted]*\n523:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings: Arrangement and Optionality\n574:**Q4 — B.** The scheduler notifies the API server of its decision; the kubelet watches the API server and acts on what it sees. Coordination in Kubernetes is watching, not telling.\n587:🌊 **Chapter 3 · Voyage Progress:** census complete → arrangement complete → now the loop\n601:**In Kubernetes, controllers are control loops that watch the state of your cluster, then make or request changes where needed. Each controller tries to move the current cluster state closer to the desired state** [source: k8s-docs-controllers-2026-08-23].\n649:> A ship's company is not a workflow. There is no master schedule pinned in the wardroom listing every action the crew will take between departure and arrival, in order, with dependencies. Such a document would be worthless within the hour, because the sea does not consult it.\n651:[Omitted long matching line]\n653:> The vessel arrives on course not because someone executed a plan but because a few dozen small corrections were made continuously by people who were each watching one thing. No one aboard is running the voyage. The voyage is what all of that watching adds up to.\n667:Now go back to Soundings question 1: three copies, you want five. The script answer works exactly once, in exactly the conditions you wrote it for. The loop answer handles the same request, plus the machine that dies at 3 a.m., plus the one that dies while you're recovering from the first one, plus the copy someone deletes by hand next Tuesday, without anybody writing a rule for any of those cases. You didn't handle those cases. You stated a desired state and left something watching.\n669:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 4 — the field that holds desired state, and its status counterpart]*\n670:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 — ReplicaSet, a control loop you can watch working in real time]*\n671:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 15 — the same loop, with a Git repository holding desired state]*\n675:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings: Controllers and the Loop\n711:**Q1 — B.** Controllers are control loops that watch cluster state and make or request changes, each trying to move current state closer to desired state [source: k8s-docs-controllers-2026-08-23].\n739:🌅 **Chapter 3 · Voyage Progress:** census → arrangement → **loop complete**\n749:From §5: all state moves through one hub, and components coordinate by watching that state rather than by instructing each other. From §6: many independent loops each watch that state and act on their own account, continuously, without terminating.\n767:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 1 §2 — where this book first called Kubernetes an orchestrator, in the industry's loose sense]*\n783:You now own a shape. Desired state, current state, an action closing the gap, repeating without end. You will meet it again in Chapter 6, where a ReplicaSet lets you watch it work in real time. In Chapter 15, where the desired state lives in a Git repository outside the cluster and the loop reaches in, which will look like a completely new technology until you recognize it. And in Chapter 17, where it turns out to be one of the things \"cloud native\" means.\n787:🏆 **Safe Harbor reached — the architecture is behind you.**\n789:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 — controllers you configure yourself]*\n790:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 15 — the loop, pointed somewhere unexpected]*\n791:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 17 — the same pattern, named as a principle]*\n803:5. **What Kubernetes is not.** Not a traditional all-inclusive PaaS. Does not build your source. Does not ship middleware, databases, or caches. Does not mandate logging or configuration solutions.\n1003:**Q12 — B.** The object exists as a stored description; something has to be watching for it and willing to act, and if that component is absent, nothing acts. This is the absent-component pattern from §4.\n1024:- **A, C, D are wrong** — plausible-sounding pairs that appear nowhere in the documentation. If any of them felt right, that's worth noticing: real terminology and invented terminology read identically until you've anchored the real pair.\n1048:| **Origin** | Descended from Google's Borg (and its research successor Omega). Written in Go. First commit 2014-06-06. v1.0 July 2015. CNCF's first donated project. Name = Greek for helmsman. |\n1060:| **The hub** | Everything goes through the API server. Only the API server reaches etcd. Coordination is watching, not telling. |\n1068:## The Voyage Ahead\n1070:You've met the crew and you know how they coordinate. What you don't yet have is the thing they're all watching.\n1072:Every component in this chapter reads from and writes to the same shared state, and that state is made of *objects*, the descriptions you submit and the system maintains. §6 kept saying \"the field that says what should be true\" and cross-bearing forward, because that field has a name, and a counterpart that reports what actually is true, and a set of conventions for how you write both. Chapter 4 gives them to you.\n1074:That's when the control loop stops being an architecture diagram and becomes something you operate. You'll write a description, submit it, and watch a loop you now understand go to work on it. The reason that will feel unremarkable rather than magical is that you did the work in this chapter first.\n1078:> *\"The vessel arrives on course not because someone executed a plan, but because a hundred small corrections were made continuously by people who were each watching one thing.\"*"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cb1a9aa5-9841-4b66-9433-eaa658323994","uuid":"5fef1373-768f-4395-80fd-1245b36c1b9a","timestamp":"2026-08-24T07:27:35.044Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"1:# Chapter 3: The Ship's Company\n2:## *\"Everyone aboard has one job, and none of them is 'be in charge'\"*\n17:| ☆ Taking Your Bearings #1 | 6 min | Medium | After a brief break |\n20:| ☆ Taking Your Bearings #2 | 5 min | Medium | After a brief break |\n22:| ☆ Taking Your Bearings #3 | 5 min | Medium | After a brief break |\n30:*If you only have 15 minutes: read §6 and take Taking Your Bearings #3. Six later chapters retrieve §6 by name. Nothing else in this chapter is retrieved as often.*\n39:## 🧭 Soundings\n118:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 2 §4 — the kubelet, CRI, and the container runtime chain]*\n154:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 2 §1 — the architectural container-versus-VM contrast]*\n192:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 3 §7 — the orchestration disclaimer, resolved]*\n202:The name comes from the Greek word for helmsman or pilot; \"K8s\" is the numeronym, with eight letters between the K and the s [source: k8s-history-ten-years-2026-08-23]. The brand you're reading did not pick the maritime register to be cute about it. The subject arrived that way.\n206:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 17 §1 — the CNCF, its governance, and the cloud native definition]*\n216:So: two kinds of machine, two sets of software. That answers Soundings question 3, and it's the first thing to fix in your head, because every component name you're about to learn hangs off it.\n254:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 3 §5 — what \"front end\" actually implies about the cluster's shape]*\n260:[Omitted long matching line]\n264:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 8 — etcd backup and restore in practice]*\n265:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 12 — Secrets live in etcd, which is why encryption at rest is a separate decision you have to make]*\n273:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 7 — how the scheduler actually chooses, in detail]*\n277:[Omitted long matching line]\n279:[Omitted long matching line]\n293:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 2 §4 — the kubelet, CRI, and container runtime chain, which this section places in its architectural context]*\n303:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 5 — Pods, PodSpecs, and what \"running and healthy\" means precisely]*\n307:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 13 §5 — `crictl`, and why a node-level tool exists below the Kubernetes API]*\n315:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 9 — Services, and how kube-proxy implements them]*\n333:[Omitted long matching line]\n337:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings: The Ship's Company\n392:- **B and C are wrong** — plausible-sounding Kubernetes-flavored packaging that the documentation never describes. If you picked one of these, the useful lesson is that \"sounds like how Kubernetes would do it\" is not a reliable guide.\n404:**If you scored 0–2:** Go back to §2 and §3 and rebuild the census from the ★ Fixed Point, then re-read the Dead Reckoning block. Ten minutes. The rest of the chapter assumes you can place any component on the right side of the control-plane/node line without thinking about it.\n414:🗺️ **Chapter 3 · Voyage Progress:** census complete → now the arrangement → then the loop\n438:> ⚠ **Navigational Hazards:** Two very common wrong beliefs live here, and they're both cheap points on exam day.\n454:[Omitted long matching line]\n456:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 9 — CoreDNS as the cluster DNS addon, and the Service DNS records it serves]*\n457:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 10 — an Ingress with no Ingress controller does nothing at all: the same pattern, first recurrence]*\n458:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 13 — `kubectl top` with no metrics-server installed]*\n459:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 17 — VPA is an add-on and is not shipped by default]*\n490:[Omitted long matching line]\n498:Now consider what that means for two components that appear to cooperate. When the scheduler assigns a Pod to a node and the kubelet on that node starts the containers, it *looks* like a handoff. It looks like the scheduler told the kubelet to do something. It didn't. The scheduler recorded a decision. The kubelet, independently, was watching for exactly that kind of record, saw it, and acted. Neither one knows the other exists.\n500:> ⚓ **Worth Securing:** The coordination mechanism in Kubernetes is **watching, not telling.** Components don't send each other instructions. They observe shared state and act on what they see. That single sentence explains most of what looks mysterious about Kubernetes' behavior, and it's the sentence Chapter 15 will retrieve when a tool called Argo CD sits outside the cluster watching a Git repository, because that's the same architecture with a different thing in the hub position.\n502:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 15 — the same shape, with a Git repository where etcd sits here]*\n509:2. From that moment, the description is simply part of the cluster's state, visible to anything watching.\n512:5. Status flows back the same way, through the API server, into etcd, where anything watching can see it.\n518:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 8 — the authentication, authorization, and admission gates a request actually passes through on its way in]*\n519:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 4 — how you write the description that gets submitted]*\n523:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings: Arrangement and Optionality\n574:**Q4 — B.** The scheduler notifies the API server of its decision; the kubelet watches the API server and acts on what it sees. Coordination in Kubernetes is watching, not telling.\n587:🌊 **Chapter 3 · Voyage Progress:** census complete → arrangement complete → now the loop\n601:**In Kubernetes, controllers are control loops that watch the state of your cluster, then make or request changes where needed. Each controller tries to move the current cluster state closer to the desired state** [source: k8s-docs-controllers-2026-08-23].\n649:> A ship's company is not a workflow. There is no master schedule pinned in the wardroom listing every action the crew will take between departure and arrival, in order, with dependencies. Such a document would be worthless within the hour, because the sea does not consult it.\n651:[Omitted long matching line]\n653:> The vessel arrives on course not because someone executed a plan but because a few dozen small corrections were made continuously by people who were each watching one thing. No one aboard is running the voyage. The voyage is what all of that watching adds up to.\n667:Now go back to Soundings question 1: three copies, you want five. The script answer works exactly once, in exactly the conditions you wrote it for. The loop answer handles the same request, plus the machine that dies at 3 a.m., plus the one that dies while you're recovering from the first one, plus the copy someone deletes by hand next Tuesday, without anybody writing a rule for any of those cases. You didn't handle those cases. You stated a desired state and left something watching.\n669:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 4 — the field that holds desired state, and its status counterpart]*\n670:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 — ReplicaSet, a control loop you can watch working in real time]*\n671:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 15 — the same loop, with a Git repository holding desired state]*\n675:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings: Controllers and the Loop\n711:**Q1 — B.** Controllers are control loops that watch cluster state and make or request changes, each trying to move current state closer to desired state [source: k8s-docs-controllers-2026-08-23].\n739:🌅 **Chapter 3 · Voyage Progress:** census → arrangement → **loop complete**\n749:From §5: all state moves through one hub, and components coordinate by watching that state rather than by instructing each other. From §6: many independent loops each watch that state and act on their own account, continuously, without terminating.\n767:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 1 §2 — where this book first called Kubernetes an orchestrator, in the industry's loose sense]*\n783:You now own a shape. Desired state, current state, an action closing the gap, repeating without end. You will meet it again in Chapter 6, where a ReplicaSet lets you watch it work in real time. In Chapter 15, where the desired state lives in a Git repository outside the cluster and the loop reaches in, which will look like a completely new technology until you recognize it. And in Chapter 17, where it turns out to be one of the things \"cloud native\" means.\n787:🏆 **Safe Harbor reached — the architecture is behind you.**\n789:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 — controllers you configure yourself]*\n790:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 15 — the loop, pointed somewhere unexpected]*\n791:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 17 — the same pattern, named as a principle]*\n803:5. **What Kubernetes is not.** Not a traditional all-inclusive PaaS. Does not build your source. Does not ship middleware, databases, or caches. Does not mandate logging or configuration solutions.\n1003:**Q12 — B.** The object exists as a stored description; something has to be watching for it and willing to act, and if that component is absent, nothing acts. This is the absent-component pattern from §4.\n1024:- **A, C, D are wrong** — plausible-sounding pairs that appear nowhere in the documentation. If any of them felt right, that's worth noticing: real terminology and invented terminology read identically until you've anchored the real pair.\n1048:| **Origin** | Descended from Google's Borg (and its research successor Omega). Written in Go. First commit 2014-06-06. v1.0 July 2015. CNCF's first donated project. Name = Greek for helmsman. |\n1060:| **The hub** | Everything goes through the API server. Only the API server reaches etcd. Coordination is watching, not telling. |\n1068:## The Voyage Ahead\n1070:You've met the crew and you know how they coordinate. What you don't yet have is the thing they're all watching.\n1072:Every component in this chapter reads from and writes to the same shared state, and that state is made of *objects*, the descriptions you submit and the system maintains. §6 kept saying \"the field that says what should be true\" and cross-bearing forward, because that field has a name, and a counterpart that reports what actually is true, and a set of conventions for how you write both. Chapter 4 gives them to you.\n1074:That's when the control loop stops being an architecture diagram and becomes something you operate. You'll write a description, submit it, and watch a loop you now understand go to work on it. The reason that will feel unremarkable rather than magical is that you did the work in this chapter first.\n1078:> *\"The vessel arrives on course not because someone executed a plan, but because a hundred small corrections were made continuously by people who were each watching one thing.\"*","numLines":74,"totalLines":74}}
